  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "social-explanation",
      "title": "What sociology tries to explain",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 10
        },
        {
          "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
          "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
          "page": 10
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Sociology studies human social life, groups, institutions and societies. Its focus is how social influences shape behaviour, experience and interpretation. This does not require ignoring the individual; it asks what an explanation misses if it considers only an individual trait.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 10
            },
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The decks use school performance as an example: instead of treating a result as proof of intelligence or effort alone, investigate how family, education, peers and opportunity may be connected. These are variables to examine, not automatic explanations already proved.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 12
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 13
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 18
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Sociology connects individual behaviour and experience with social influences, groups, institutions and wider society."
    },
    {
      "id": "vocabulary",
      "title": "Norms, values, beliefs and traditions",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 7
        },
        {
          "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
          "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
          "page": 7
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A norm is an accepted expectation or rule guiding behaviour in a group. A value is a shared standard of what is good, right or fair. A belief is something accepted as true; a tradition is a practice or belief passed between generations. The terms are related but answer different questions.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 7
            },
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 7
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The culture task asks students to combine these ideas into a definition. As a didactic synthesis, culture can be described here through shared norms, values, beliefs and traditions. That synthesis is a response to the task, not a verbatim definition already filled in on the slide.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 7
            },
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 7
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 8
            },
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 8
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "comparison": {
        "columns": [
          "Concept",
          "Question it helps answer"
        ],
        "rows": [
          {
            "label": "Norm",
            "values": [
              "Expected behaviour",
              "What are people expected to do?"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Value",
            "values": [
              "Shared standard",
              "What is regarded as good or fair?"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Belief",
            "values": [
              "Accepted idea",
              "What do people take to be true?"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Tradition",
            "values": [
              "Transmission over generations",
              "What is passed on?"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "summary": "Distinguish norms, values, beliefs and traditions by asking whether a term describes expectations, standards, truth or continuity."
    },
    {
      "id": "institutions",
      "title": "Institutions connect roles and expectations",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 11
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 12
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The decks describe institutions as organised social arrangements, rather than merely buildings. Family, government, economy, education and religion structure relationships and expectations. Family is linked to primary socialisation, the economy to goods and services, education to transmitting knowledge, and government to legitimate power.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 11
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 12
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Social structure consists of the institutions and relationships forming society’s building blocks. To explain an educational pattern, ask how institutions connect: for example, how family circumstances and school expectations might interact.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 11
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 12
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Family, government, economy, education and religion organise social roles and expectations rather than merely occupying buildings."
    },
    {
      "id": "common-sense",
      "title": "An ordinary impression is a hypothesis, not evidence",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 14
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 15
        },
        {
          "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
          "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
          "page": 16
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Common-sense ideas may feel obvious because they are familiar. The decks show that such beliefs vary across time and societies and may reproduce prejudice. Sociology asks whether evidence supports an explanation and considers alternatives before accepting it.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 14
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 15
            },
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 16
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The shorter deck explicitly stresses evidence, objectivity and keeping an open mind. The exercise about a silent student demonstrates the method: do not jump from observed silence to an identity label; consider past experiences, peer reactions and the meaning of the classroom setting.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 16
            },
            {
              "id": "ac36a90c3ad6b14b4d69a4d82748eaae267ab51f49cea7286493b7987fd1a9b6",
              "sha256": "5561fee6afa143a6dfbd35687c6a947ae93025653c24c1aaa299fc2cbf085c6b",
              "page": 17
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 17
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Describe the observation",
          "text": "Record the behaviour without turning it into an unsupported character judgement.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 17
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 18
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Identify the first explanation",
          "text": "Name the common-sense assumption so it can be questioned.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 17
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 18
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Generate alternatives",
          "text": "Consider at least two social variables: peers, family, opportunities or past experience.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 17
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 18
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Seek evidence",
          "text": "Distinguish a possible explanation from one supported by observation.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 17
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 18
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Treat familiar impressions as claims to test with evidence and alternative explanations, rather than accepting them as obvious truths."
    },
    {
      "id": "imagination",
      "title": "Connect personal experience with social structures",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 19
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 20
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The longer deck links C. Wright Mills and sociological imagination to the ability to connect life experiences with wider structures and institutions. Socialisation is the process through which people learn how to behave from institutions and interactions throughout life.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 19
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 20
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The waiting-room task asks which unwritten rules people follow and who taught them. The point is to notice learned expectations that may feel natural only because they are familiar. The slides challenge a purely biological explanation; they do not prove that biology never matters.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 19
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 20
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 22
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 23
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 24
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "summary": "Sociological imagination connects personal experience with wider structures, while socialisation explains how expectations are learned."
    },
    {
      "id": "perspectives",
      "title": "Three questions about the same behaviour",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 25
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 28
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 30
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 31
        },
        {
          "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
          "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
          "page": 33
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In the course’s structuralist perspective, focus on institutions, rules and expectations that shape or constrain behaviour. The functionalist perspective asks what those arrangements contribute to society. Social action focuses on individual meanings and motivations; symbolic interactionism examines meanings formed through interaction.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 25
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 28
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 30
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 31
            },
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 33
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The café example holds the observed behaviour constant: people queue. A structuralist question concerns the norm of waiting, a functionalist question concerns orderly service, and a social-action question asks why a particular person waits. Different perspectives select different explanatory questions rather than simply giving different labels to the same answer.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 33
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "comparison": {
        "columns": [
          "Perspective",
          "Question in the course"
        ],
        "rows": [
          {
            "label": "Structuralism",
            "values": [
              "Rules, roles and constraints",
              "What arrangement shapes behaviour?"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Functionalism",
            "values": [
              "Contribution to society",
              "What does this arrangement do?"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Social action",
            "values": [
              "Meaning and motivation",
              "What does the action mean to the individual?"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Observe a queue",
          "text": "Describe who waits and what pattern appears.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 33
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Change the lens",
          "text": "Ask about the rule, its function and the actor’s motivation separately.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 33
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Compare explanations",
          "text": "A habit, fairness belief and avoiding confrontation are possibilities to investigate, not facts proved about every person.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "5d233eb1658c11a6bd14dc23c41e51ecc2d67b9423830cbee78692fa9ebfa6d8",
              "sha256": "c7f7911ec47050fe1c17f307d7e52bedca4e4ceb5f5d670feb60d018c3083635",
              "page": 33
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
